# Journeys run one at a time

Always replay with `--sequential`. Never pass `--parallel`, and never raise
`--concurrency` above 1.

## Why

The model endpoint rate-limits well below what six concurrent conversations
ask of it. When it does, every affected turn returns

    I encountered an error while helping with your shopping request.

which the checks score as a normal failure. The report then reads as a quality
collapse rather than an infrastructure one: 60 scenarios "finished" in five
minutes, 176 of 257 turns errored on `openai.RateLimitError: Error code: 429`,
and every journey showed `0 products shown`.

Nothing in the report says "rate limited". The only way to tell a void run from
a real regression is to go back to the container logs, so the cost of the
mistake is paid twice -- once in the wasted run, once in diagnosing it.

## Reading a suspect run

Before treating failures as regressions, check:

- **Wall-clock.** A full sequential pass is roughly an hour. Anything that
  finishes in minutes did not run.
- **The reply text** in `raw/*.json`. The generic error above means the turn
  never reached the agent.
- `docker logs chain-server --timestamps | rg "RateLimitError"`, scoped to the
  run's window. Note `--since` is relative to now, so an overnight run needs a
  timestamp filter rather than `--since 6h`.

## Comparing across runs

Timings are only comparable at equal concurrency, which is another reason to
keep it at 1 always. Every stored baseline under
`tests/evaluation/results/val/` was produced at concurrency 1.
